1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,640
Early one morning in September nineteen forty-five,

2
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:06,360
a German prisoner of war sat on an American bus

3
00:00:06,209 --> 00:00:09,839
with three dollars and seventy-five cents in his pocket.

4
00:00:10,689 --> 00:00:12,919
He had walked out of a guarded camp a few

5
00:00:12,769 --> 00:00:16,459
hours earlier. Nobody on the bus knew it.

6
00:00:16,989 --> 00:00:18,539
He wore ordinary clothes,

7
00:00:18,690 --> 00:00:19,779
he spoke good English,

8
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:22,160
and he kept his face turned toward the window.

9
00:00:23,324 --> 00:00:26,254
For six years, the cities he knew had gone dark

10
00:00:26,105 --> 00:00:29,114
at night. Since the first day of the war,

11
00:00:29,364 --> 00:00:31,714
German streets had been blacked out against bombers.

12
00:00:32,804 --> 00:00:36,975
Windows were covered. Streetlamps were hooded or switched off.

13
00:00:37,865 --> 00:00:39,454
A lit window could bring a fine,

14
00:00:39,304 --> 00:00:40,115
or worse.

15
00:00:41,274 --> 00:00:43,804
Then the road reached the edge of Chicago.

16
00:00:44,734 --> 00:00:47,585
He later wrote that a million lights popped up everywhere.

17
00:00:49,225 --> 00:00:51,375
His name was Reinhold Pabel.

18
00:00:52,204 --> 00:00:54,975
He told the story ten years later in a memoir

19
00:00:54,825 --> 00:00:56,554
called Enemies Are Human.

20
00:00:57,484 --> 00:01:00,594
A single lit skyline proves very little on its own.

21
00:01:01,365 --> 00:01:04,415
But Pabel had been watching America through windows for almost

22
00:01:04,265 --> 00:01:07,255
two years, and that night was the end of a

23
00:01:07,105 --> 00:01:09,515
long argument he had been having with himself.

24
00:01:11,099 --> 00:01:14,490
Pabel was born in Hamburg during the First World War.

25
00:01:15,299 --> 00:01:18,210
He studied, travelled, and wanted to become a pastor.

26
00:01:19,299 --> 00:01:22,230
In nineteen forty he was drafted into the army.

27
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:24,930
He fought in the invasion of Russia,

28
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:26,810
was wounded in the first winter,

29
00:01:26,659 --> 00:01:30,390
and in nineteen forty-three was sent south to Sicily and

30
00:01:30,239 --> 00:01:31,810
then to the Italian mainland.

31
00:01:33,075 --> 00:01:34,585
Near the Volturno River,

32
00:01:34,875 --> 00:01:37,685
his squad stumbled into an American aid station.

33
00:01:37,534 --> 00:01:42,844
Pabel spoke English. He started talking with a lieutenant from

34
00:01:42,694 --> 00:01:45,445
Dallas named Paul Lindsey.

35
00:01:45,295 --> 00:01:46,265
For a few minutes,

36
00:01:46,355 --> 00:01:48,445
two enemies compared notes on the war.

37
00:01:49,394 --> 00:01:52,765
Soon after, Pabel was badly wounded jumping a dam under

38
00:01:52,614 --> 00:01:55,865
fire. He crawled back to the same Americans,

39
00:01:56,034 --> 00:01:57,825
and their medics saved his life.

40
00:01:58,974 --> 00:02:02,045
By the end of November nineteen forty-three he was in

41
00:02:01,894 --> 00:02:04,365
a prisoner of war camp in North Africa.

42
00:02:05,254 --> 00:02:07,385
Then came a ship across the Atlantic.

43
00:02:08,414 --> 00:02:10,237
On the morning of January second,

44
00:02:10,087 --> 00:02:13,205
nineteen forty-four, the prisoners went ashore at Norfolk,

45
00:02:13,615 --> 00:02:13,784
Virginia.

46
00:02:15,449 --> 00:02:18,020
The guards put them on a train heading west.

47
00:02:17,870 --> 00:02:21,620
Pabel wrote that, crossing Virginia and Kentucky,

48
00:02:22,009 --> 00:02:25,300
the men pressed their noses against the windowpanes.

49
00:02:25,150 --> 00:02:28,599
The first thing they noticed was the abundance of automobiles

50
00:02:28,449 --> 00:02:31,360
everywhere. Then the frame houses,

51
00:02:31,770 --> 00:02:33,000
mile after mile of them.

52
00:02:34,099 --> 00:02:36,550
When a porter came through the carriage with coffee and

53
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:38,530
sandwiches, and offered them politely,

54
00:02:38,379 --> 00:02:40,830
Pabel wrote that most of the men forgot,

55
00:02:41,139 --> 00:02:44,250
for a while, the anti-American feelings they had brought with

56
00:02:44,099 --> 00:02:44,510
them.

57
00:02:45,574 --> 00:02:47,625
Their destination was Camp Grant,

58
00:02:47,474 --> 00:02:52,465
near Rockford, Illinois. It was Pabel's first permanent camp: barracks,

59
00:02:52,634 --> 00:02:54,385
wire, watch towers, and a library.

60
00:02:55,354 --> 00:02:56,805
He took language courses.

61
00:02:57,775 --> 00:02:59,564
He read American newspapers.

62
00:03:00,634 --> 00:03:03,545
Later he was chosen to translate and read out the

63
00:03:03,395 --> 00:03:05,125
daily news to the other prisoners.

64
00:03:06,775 --> 00:03:09,205
The news from home was getting worse.

65
00:03:10,235 --> 00:03:12,825
Hamburg had been struck by a series of raids in

66
00:03:12,675 --> 00:03:16,344
the summer of nineteen forty-three that started a firestorm and

67
00:03:16,194 --> 00:03:18,245
killed tens of thousands of people.

68
00:03:18,094 --> 00:03:22,504
Pabel received a telegram saying his family had been bombed

69
00:03:22,354 --> 00:03:25,284
out again. It was the second time.

70
00:03:26,375 --> 00:03:28,825
In Germany, darkness was not a mood.

71
00:03:29,414 --> 00:03:30,705
It was the law.

72
00:03:31,775 --> 00:03:34,265
Blackout rules had come into force on the first day

73
00:03:34,115 --> 00:03:35,664
of the war in nineteen thirty-nine,

74
00:03:35,514 --> 00:03:37,564
and the night was for sirens,

75
00:03:37,735 --> 00:03:41,784
cellars and searchlights. Men who had lived under those rules

76
00:03:41,634 --> 00:03:44,004
for years learned to notice light.

77
00:03:45,125 --> 00:03:47,275
America had its own restrictions.

78
00:03:48,344 --> 00:03:51,974
In nineteen forty-two the coastal cities were dimmed so that

79
00:03:51,824 --> 00:03:54,955
ships offshore would not be silhouetted for German submarines.

80
00:03:56,085 --> 00:03:59,694
In early nineteen forty-five a national dim-out to save fuel

81
00:03:59,544 --> 00:04:03,135
switched off neon signs and dimmed street lighting across the

82
00:04:02,985 --> 00:04:03,414
country.

83
00:04:04,599 --> 00:04:06,530
But a dim-out was not a blackout.

84
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:10,189
Nobody in Illinois was sheltering from bombers.

85
00:04:11,199 --> 00:04:12,310
And on V E Day,

86
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:14,590
May eighth, nineteen forty-five,

87
00:04:14,439 --> 00:04:15,590
even the dim-out ended.

88
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:18,929
The war in Europe was over,

89
00:04:18,879 --> 00:04:21,109
but the prisoners were not going home.

90
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:23,669
After May, men at Camp Ellis,

91
00:04:23,839 --> 00:04:25,149
where Pabel had been moved,

92
00:04:25,279 --> 00:04:27,069
were told to volunteer for work.

93
00:04:27,939 --> 00:04:30,870
In the first week of September nineteen forty-five,

94
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:33,029
he was sent to a small branch camp at Washington,

95
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:36,469
Illinois, where a cannery needed hands for the corn season.

96
00:04:37,675 --> 00:04:39,704
It was not much of a fortress.

97
00:04:39,555 --> 00:04:43,564
Pabel noticed that only one watch tower had been put

98
00:04:43,415 --> 00:04:46,524
up. Peoria was nineteen miles away,

99
00:04:46,375 --> 00:04:49,484
and from Peoria a bus line ran to Chicago.

100
00:04:50,454 --> 00:04:52,704
He asked to switch from the noon shift to the

101
00:04:52,555 --> 00:04:54,984
night shift, saying he did not feel well.

102
00:04:55,814 --> 00:04:57,044
During the change of shifts,

103
00:04:56,894 --> 00:04:57,924
he walked away.

104
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:03,529
An Associated Press report dated September 11th carried the headline,

105
00:05:03,379 --> 00:05:05,969
War Prisoner Flees Camp Near Peoria.

106
00:05:06,839 --> 00:05:09,089
The camp commander announced the escape.

107
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:11,909
By then Pabel was already gone.

108
00:05:13,449 --> 00:05:16,299
A driver gave him a lift on the highway to

109
00:05:16,149 --> 00:05:19,539
Peoria. At the bus depot on the public square,

110
00:05:19,810 --> 00:05:22,319
he waited, trying not to look like a man who

111
00:05:22,170 --> 00:05:24,079
had never bought a ticket in America.

112
00:05:25,110 --> 00:05:27,599
At two o'clock in the morning he asked for one

113
00:05:27,449 --> 00:05:31,479
ticket to Chicago. It cost more than half of everything

114
00:05:31,329 --> 00:05:33,620
he had. While he waited,

115
00:05:33,730 --> 00:05:35,500
he ate eggs and drank coffee.

116
00:05:36,009 --> 00:05:38,539
He called it his first meal in liberty.

117
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:42,189
He wrote that with every mile the coach ate up,

118
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:44,750
he came one mile closer to his new life.

119
00:05:45,899 --> 00:05:48,109
Fields and small towns passed in the dark.

120
00:05:48,939 --> 00:05:50,089
Then came the suburbs,

121
00:05:49,939 --> 00:05:50,929
and the lights.

122
00:05:52,074 --> 00:05:55,044
In Hamburg, a city that size at night would have

123
00:05:54,894 --> 00:05:58,164
been a target. Here it was simply a city.

124
00:05:59,095 --> 00:06:01,404
Nobody was hiding anything from the sky.

125
00:06:02,550 --> 00:06:06,419
Downtown, Pabel saw brightly coloured lights and window displays.

126
00:06:07,449 --> 00:06:10,000
He later described the giant Merchandise Mart,

127
00:06:09,850 --> 00:06:13,219
with its thousand lights reflected in the Chicago River.

128
00:06:14,129 --> 00:06:15,739
So this is my new home,

129
00:06:15,790 --> 00:06:19,459
he wrote. Bewildered, he got off the bus on Randolph

130
00:06:19,310 --> 00:06:19,839
Street.

131
00:06:21,375 --> 00:06:23,404
Freedom did not make him safe.

132
00:06:24,214 --> 00:06:26,044
An escaped prisoner had no papers,

133
00:06:26,295 --> 00:06:28,504
no history and no right to a job.

134
00:06:29,555 --> 00:06:30,604
Pabel took a new name,

135
00:06:30,754 --> 00:06:32,544
Phil Brick, short and forgettable.

136
00:06:33,415 --> 00:06:35,564
He washed dishes in Chicago restaurants,

137
00:06:35,915 --> 00:06:37,484
one of them on North Clark Street,

138
00:06:37,694 --> 00:06:38,844
and saved what he could.

139
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,109
Most escapes ended in days.

140
00:06:43,079 --> 00:06:46,049
The F B I published advice on how fugitives were

141
00:06:45,899 --> 00:06:48,849
caught, and Pabel had read it in the camp library.

142
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:52,509
He tried to avoid every mistake it described.

143
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:55,829
He saved enough to buy a used bookshop on the

144
00:06:55,680 --> 00:06:57,849
North Side, the Chicago Book Mart.

145
00:06:58,660 --> 00:07:01,289
He married an American woman named Avis.

146
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:03,269
They had a child.

147
00:07:04,449 --> 00:07:06,412
On the morning of March ninth,

148
00:07:06,262 --> 00:07:09,819
nineteen fifty-three, a customer walked into the shop and asked

149
00:07:09,670 --> 00:07:13,560
for Reinhold Pabel. The customer was an F B I

150
00:07:13,410 --> 00:07:16,419
agent. After almost eight years,

151
00:07:16,569 --> 00:07:17,659
the escape was over.

152
00:07:19,199 --> 00:07:21,469
The case became national news.

153
00:07:22,379 --> 00:07:24,029
The government wanted him deported,

154
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:25,429
but there was a problem.

155
00:07:25,279 --> 00:07:28,789
Pabel had not entered the country illegally.

156
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:32,469
The United States Army had brought him in.

157
00:07:33,079 --> 00:07:34,649
Old friends spoke up for him,

158
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:36,449
among them Paul Lindsey,

159
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:37,969
the lieutenant from the aid station,

160
00:07:38,139 --> 00:07:41,129
now a lawyer. Pabel left for Germany,

161
00:07:41,220 --> 00:07:42,509
waited about six months,

162
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:44,629
and then returned legally to his wife,

163
00:07:44,860 --> 00:07:46,310
his child and his bookshop.

164
00:07:47,399 --> 00:07:50,870
In his memoir he insisted that propaganda of any kind,

165
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:52,949
German or American, does harm.

166
00:07:53,879 --> 00:07:56,209
What changed his mind was not a pamphlet.

167
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:58,469
It was coffee from a porter,

168
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:00,989
a library, and the view from a bus window.

169
00:08:02,175 --> 00:08:05,044
If you know another prisoner account we should look into,

170
00:08:05,295 --> 00:08:06,264
put it in the comments.

171
00:08:07,875 --> 00:08:10,264
A blackout can hide a city from bombers.

172
00:08:11,095 --> 00:08:13,964
It can also hide a country from its own soldiers.

173
00:08:15,074 --> 00:08:17,924
Reinhold Pabel came from a country that had spent six

174
00:08:17,774 --> 00:08:18,944
years in the dark.

175
00:08:19,675 --> 00:08:20,924
At the edge of Chicago,

176
00:08:21,314 --> 00:08:23,024
a million lights popped up everywhere,

177
00:08:22,875 --> 00:08:24,864
and nobody bothered to cover them.
